---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const slug = 'morph-api-review-2026';
const date = '2026-08-28';
const locale = 'en';
const enTitle = 'Morph API Review 2026: Kimi K3, GLM-5.2 & Fast Apply';
const enDescription = 'Morph LLM API 2026 review: Fast Apply at 10,500 tok/s for code merges, Compact context compression, WarpGrep code search, Kimi K3 + GLM-5.2 + Qwen 3.5 397B inference, OpenAI + Anthropic wire formats, MCP server for Claude Code.';
const canonicalUrl = `https://apirank.vip/tutorials/${slug}`;
const zhCanonicalUrl = `https://apirank.vip/zh/tutorials/${slug}`;

const articleJsonLd = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'Article', headline: enTitle, description: enDescription, datePublished: date, dateModified: date, inLanguage: 'en-US', author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'APIRank', url: 'https://apirank.vip' }, publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: 'APIRank', logo: { '@type': 'ImageObject', url: 'https://apirank.vip/favicon.svg' }}, mainEntityOfPage: { '@type': 'WebPage', '@id': canonicalUrl } };
const breadcrumbJsonLd = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [{ '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://apirank.vip' }, { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Tutorials', item: 'https://apirank.vip/tutorials' }, { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: enTitle, item: canonicalUrl }] };
const faqJsonLd = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: [
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is Morph and what does the API do?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Morph (morphllm.com) is a Y Combinator-backed inference API positioned as 'inference built for agents.' It ships specialized endpoints for the agent loop — Fast Apply (10,500 tok/s code edits at 96-98% accuracy), Compact (33,000 tok/s context compression with byte-for-byte keepContext preservation), WarpGrep (semantic code search in ~6 s), Reflexes (11 built-in text classifiers in ~90 ms), and Model Router (prompt difficulty classifier at $0.005/request) — plus an open-weight chat lineup (Kimi K3, GLM-5.2, Qwen 3.5 397B, DeepSeek V4 Flash, MiniMax M3 / M2.7, Qwen 3.8 / 3.6 27B, Gemma 4 31B) on both the OpenAI Chat Completions and Anthropic Messages wire formats. Base URL https://api.morphllm.com, one API key." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'How much does the Morph API cost?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Morph is pure pay-as-you-go across the board, no monthly subscription, no permanent free tier. Per-1M token rates for open-weight chat (verified 2026-08-28 from docs.morphllm.com/llms.txt): Kimi K3 $2.90/$14.00, Qwen 3.5 397B $0.50/$3.50 (cached input $0.30/M), GLM-5.2 744B $1.10/$4.10, GLM-5.3-Flash $0.15/$0.50, MiniMax M3 428B $0.30/$1.20, DeepSeek V4 Flash beta $0.12/$0.278, Qwen 3.8 / 3.6 27B $0.289/$2.40, Gemma 4 31B $0.14/$0.40. Fast Apply via OpenRouter-verified morph-v3-fast $0.0008/$0.0012 per 1k tokens and morph-v3-large $0.0009/$0.0019 per 1k tokens. Reflexes bill per event: realtime $0.001/event (drops to $0.0005 past 1M events/month), async batch $0.0005/event (drops to $0.00025 past 1M). Model Router is $0.005 per request flat. Up to $5,000 in Startup Credits available for qualified startups via a separate sales contact form." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Is Morph OpenAI-compatible?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Yes for chat and for every open-weight chat model. The base URL is https://api.morphllm.com/v1 with the standard OpenAI Chat Completions surface, so the official OpenAI Python and TypeScript SDKs work as drop-in by changing base_url. Every chat model also serves the Anthropic Messages wire format at /v1/messages with /v1/messages/count_tokens support, so the official Anthropic SDKs work too — and Claude Code / Codex can be pointed at Morph with four environment variables (ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL, ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN, ANTHROPIC_MODEL, ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL). Specialized endpoints (Fast Apply, Compact, WarpGrep, Reflexes, Model Router) are OpenAI-format only." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is Fast Apply and how does it work?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Fast Apply merges AI-generated code edits into files at 10,500 tok/s with 96% accuracy (morph-v3-fast) or 5,000 tok/s with 98% accuracy (morph-v3-large). Use the auto model to let Morph route between them. The prompt format is proprietary: <instruction>{brief description}</instruction><code>{full original file}</code><update>{edit snippet with // ... existing code ... markers for unchanged sections}</update>. The response is a standard OpenAI chat completion with the fully merged file in choices[0].message.content (or Anthropic content[-1].text). Same models published on OpenRouter as morph/morph-v3-fast and morph/morph-v3-large." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is Compact and how does byte-for-byte preservation work?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Compact compresses chat history and code context at 33,000 tok/s — a 100k-token history compresses in under 2 seconds. Pass query to tell the model what matters for the next LLM call, compression_ratio to control how aggressively to compress, and preserve_recent to keep the last N lines verbatim. Wrap any section in <keepContext>...</keepContext> tags to guarantee byte-for-byte preservation regardless of compression ratio. Response includes output (compressed text) and compacted_line_ranges showing which lines survived. Available as POST /v1/compact (native), as model=morph-compactor on /v1/chat/completions, and on /v1/responses." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is WarpGrep?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "WarpGrep is Morph's semantic code search subagent. The model (morph-warp-grep-v2.1) has built-in tools — grep_search, read, list_directory, glob, finish — and runs a multi-turn tool-calling loop to find code locations matching a natural-language query. The caller passes <repo_structure>{flat list of absolute paths}</repo_structure> + <search_string>{natural language query}</search_string>, executes each tool_call locally on the filesystem, and replies with standard tool messages. Typical latency is ~6 seconds across 3-6 turns before the model calls finish with the code locations." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What are Reflexes?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Reflexes are 11 built-in text classifiers that label a turn in ~90 ms. Available models: jailbreak (prompt-injection detection), guardrail (NSFW/harassment), leaked-thinking (agent leaking internal reasoning), stuck-in-a-loop (agent blocked, not trying new things), incomplete-thought (truncated user prompt), user-frustrated, user-joy, ambiguity (low/med/high), difficulty (easy/medium/hard), domain (general/summary/coding/design/data), and health-emergency. Pass models as an array to run several in one request. Each event bills at the per-event rate with volume discounts past 1M events/month." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Does Morph support Claude Code?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Yes — Claude Code speaks the Anthropic Messages API, which Morph serves natively. Set ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.morphllm.com, ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN to your MORPH_API_KEY, ANTHROPIC_MODEL to morph-kimik3 (sonnet/opus remap), and ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL to morph-kimik3-fast (haiku remap). File edits, Bash, Grep tools continue to work through Anthropic's tool_use loop. Alternatively, install the MCP server with claude mcp add morph -- npx -y @morphllm/morphmcp@latest to expose edit_file, codebase_search, and github_codebase_search as native MCP tools." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'What is the Morph Model Router?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "The Model Router classifies a prompt in ~180 ms and tells you which model to call next. POST /v1/router/classify returns { difficulty, ambiguity, domain } labels with confidence and meets_threshold flags. POST /v1/router/multimodel takes allowed_models / allowed_providers / policy (balanced or cost_efficient) / default_model and returns the single best model to call next. Pricing is $0.005 per request flat. Trained on millions of coding prompts; effective for cost reduction on multi-model stacks where easy prompts go to GLM-5.3-Flash ($0.15/$0.50 per 1M) and hard prompts go to Kimi K3 or GLM-5.2." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Is there a free Morph tier?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "No permanent free tier. The Morph homepage CTA 'Start free. Get API Key' refers to signup friction — you get an API key without a credit card but you are billed pay-as-you-go from the first token. Morph offers up to $5,000 in Startup Credits for qualified startups via a separate sales contact form, applied as account credit. There is no monthly minimum, no monthly subscription, and no enterprise price list on the website." } },
  { '@type': 'Question', name: 'Does Morph work from mainland China?', acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: "Not directly. Morph runs a single global endpoint at https://api.morphllm.com with no documented Asia-Pacific regional routing. Production traffic from North America and Europe sees low single-digit tens of ms first-byte latency; mainland China requires a proxy or transit with typical 200-400 ms transpacific first-byte latency. The Fast Apply models are also published on OpenRouter as morph/morph-v3-fast and morph/morph-v3-large, which routes them through OpenRouter's own multi-region infrastructure — this gives Chinese developers a low-latency Fast Apply path without leaving the OpenRouter API key. The open-weight chat lineup is on Morph's direct API only. No ICP-licensed mainland China endpoint as of August 2026." } }
] };
const enBody = `<h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">${enTitle}</h1>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Morph</strong> (morphllm.com) is a Y Combinator-backed inference API positioned as "inference built for agents." Instead of selling yet another chat completions endpoint, Morph ships four specialized endpoints — <strong>Fast Apply</strong> (10,500 tok/s code merges), <strong>Compact</strong> (33,000 tok/s context compression), <strong>WarpGrep</strong> (semantic code search in ~6 s), <strong>Reflexes</strong> (jailbreak / loop / frustration classifiers in ~90 ms) — plus a <strong>Model Router</strong> that picks the cheapest model for each prompt, all on one OpenAI- and Anthropic-compatible API key. For an engineer building a coding agent in August 2026, Morph matters because it removes the three biggest token sinks (file edits, context compression, code search) from the general-purpose LLM bill, while serving the same Kimi K3 / GLM-5.2 / Qwen 3.5 397B open-weight lineup you'd otherwise route through Together, Fireworks, or DeepSeek. See the full <a href="/providers/morph">Morph provider page</a> for the structured comparison table.</p>

<div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-lg p-4 my-4"><strong>🌍 Quick take:</strong> Morph is the first inference API designed around the agent loop rather than chat completions. Fast Apply merges code edits at 10,500 tok/s, Compact compresses context at 33,000 tok/s with byte-for-byte preservation of <code>&lt;keepContext&gt;</code> sections, WarpGrep returns code locations in ~6 s, Reflexes classifies any agent turn in ~90 ms. One API key serves both OpenAI Chat Completions and Anthropic Messages, so Claude Code and Codex run on Kimi K3 with four env vars. No permanent free tier; up to $5K Startup Credits for qualified startups. No mainland China endpoint.</div>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">What is Morph, and why its API matters in 2026</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Morph is the public API of <strong>AutoInfra, Inc.</strong>, a Y Combinator-backed startup whose only product is a specialized inference stack for AI agents. Where OpenAI and Anthropic optimize for general chat and Anthropic's <a href="/tutorials/anthropic-claude-api-review-2026">Claude API</a> and OpenAI's GPT-5.6 lead on broad reasoning, Morph optimizes for the four operations a coding or long-running agent does on every turn:</p>

<ol class="list-decimal list-inside text-gray-700 my-4 space-y-2">
  <li><strong>Edit a file</strong> with a partial diff and return the merged file → Fast Apply at <strong>10,500 tok/s</strong> with 96-98% accuracy on the merge;</li>
  <li><strong>Compress the chat history</strong> so the next LLM call still fits in the context window → Compact at <strong>33,000 tok/s</strong> with byte-for-byte preservation of tagged sections;</li>
  <li><strong>Search the codebase</strong> for the snippet an LLM needs to answer a question → WarpGrep in <strong>~6 s</strong> with built-in <code>grep_search</code>, <code>read</code>, <code>list_directory</code>, <code>glob</code>, and <code>finish</code> tools;</li>
  <li><strong>Monitor the agent</strong> for jailbreaks, infinite loops, leaked thinking, user frustration, and prompt ambiguity → Reflexes in <strong>~90 ms</strong> per label.</li>
</ol>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">On top of those four specialized endpoints, Morph serves the open-weight chat lineup you'd expect from a 2026 inference provider — Kimi K3 (2.8T MoE, #1 on Design Arena), GLM-5.2 (744B MoE, 1M context, OpenAI <code>service_tier</code> standby), Qwen 3.5 397B (262k context), DeepSeek V4 Flash (1M context beta), MiniMax M3 / M2.7 (256k / 196k MoE), Gemma 4 31B, Qwen 3.8 / 3.6 27B — on both the OpenAI Chat Completions wire format and the Anthropic Messages wire format, so Claude Code and Codex can switch from Anthropic to open-weight models by changing four environment variables. The Model Router classifies prompt difficulty in ~180 ms and picks the cheapest model that handles it; the <a href="/tutorials/openrouter-api-review-2026">OpenRouter</a> rankings list Morph in the Top 25 by token volume, serving 100B+ tokens/day across 400+ production agents.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">The Morph product lineup in 2026</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Morph ships eight product surfaces behind one API key. Each has its own pricing model, endpoint, and SDK surface:</p>

<table class="min-w-full border border-gray-300 my-4 text-sm">
<thead><tr class="bg-gray-100">
<th class="border p-2">Product</th>
<th class="border p-2">Purpose</th>
<th class="border p-2">Billing</th>
<th class="border p-2">Best for</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td class="border p-2"><strong>Fast Apply</strong></td><td class="border p-2">Merge AI-generated code edits into files</td><td class="border p-2">Per 1k prompt+completion tokens</td><td class="border p-2">Coding agents that edit many files per turn</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border p-2"><strong>Compact</strong></td><td class="border p-2">Verbatim context compression for long chats</td><td class="border p-2">Per token / per event</td><td class="border p-2">Long-running agents with growing history</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border p-2"><strong>WarpGrep</strong></td><td class="border p-2">Semantic code search subagent</td><td class="border p-2">Per request (multi-turn)</td><td class="border p-2">Repo-aware retrieval without building your own index</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border p-2"><strong>Reflexes</strong></td><td class="border p-2">11 built-in text classifiers</td><td class="border p-2">Per event (realtime / async)</td><td class="border p-2">Production agent observability + safety</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border p-2"><strong>Model Router</strong></td><td class="border p-2">Classify prompt difficulty + route to model</td><td class="border p-2">$0.005 per request (flat)</td><td class="border p-2">Cost reduction on multi-model stacks</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border p-2"><strong>Fast Models (chat)</strong></td><td class="border p-2">Open-weight chat inference</td><td class="border p-2">Per 1M input/output tokens</td><td class="border p-2">Direct chat, Anthropic / OpenAI SDK drop-in</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border p-2"><strong>Custom Reflexes</strong></td><td class="border p-2">Train your own classifier via /v1/fine_tuning/*</td><td class="border p-2">Per fine-tuning job</td><td class="border p-2">Domain-specific guardrails (~30s train)</td></tr>
<tr><td class="border p-2"><strong>Reserved B200</strong></td><td class="border p-2">Dedicated GPU-hour capacity</td><td class="border p-2">Per GPU-hour (sales)</td><td class="border p-2">Production fleets with latency SLAs</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Every chat model also serves the Anthropic Messages wire format at <code>/v1/messages</code> with <code>/v1/messages/count_tokens</code> support, so the same API key routes Claude Code and Codex to Kimi K3, GLM-5.2, or any other chat model. Specialized endpoints (Fast Apply, Compact, WarpGrep, Reflexes, Router) are OpenAI format only.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Morph pricing: per-token, per-event, per-GPU-hour</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Morph is pay-as-you-go across the board. There is no monthly subscription, no permanent free tier, and no enterprise price list published on the website — only the per-product rates below, plus an up-to-$5,000 Startup Credits program for qualified startups (applied via a separate sales contact form). Pricing verified 2026-08-28 against <a href="https://docs.morphllm.com/llms.txt">docs.morphllm.com/llms.txt</a> (Fast Models table + Reflexes pricing section) and the OpenRouter model catalog:</p>

