<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">For a concrete cost example: a coding agent making 1,000 turns on a long task — 5 Fast Apply edits at ~2k tokens each on the <code>auto</code> route (~$0.01), 1 Compact call on a 100k-token history at the per-token rate (~$0.05), 3 WarpGrep searches (~$0.015 if at the per-request rate, or free on the unlimited tier if Morph bundles it), and 20 chat calls to Kimi K3 at ~5k input / 2k output = $0.073 per call. Daily total at 1,000 turns: under $150 — and most of that is the chat tokens, not the agent-specific endpoints, because the specialized endpoints bill per token / per event rather than per chat completion.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">How the Morph API actually works</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Morph's API is OpenAI-compatible for chat, Anthropic-compatible for chat, and OpenAI-format-only for the specialized endpoints. The base URL is <code>https://api.morphllm.com</code>; one <code>MORPH_API_KEY</code> covers every endpoint. Here's the practical flow for a coding agent built on Morph:</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Step 1: Switch the chat model.</strong> Drop the OpenAI Python SDK in, point <code>base_url</code> at <code>https://api.morphllm.com/v1</code>, and call <code>model="morph-kimik3"</code> or <code>model="morph-glm52-744b"</code>. No new SDK to learn. Anthropic SDK works the same way: <code>base_url="https://api.morphllm.com"</code> + <code>model="morph-kimik3"</code>. For Claude Code, four env vars (<code>ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL</code>, <code>ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN</code>, <code>ANTHROPIC_MODEL</code>, <code>ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL</code>) route the assistant and the small-fast model to Kimi K3 and Kimi K3 Fast respectively.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Step 2: Use Fast Apply for file edits.</strong> Send a prompt in the <code>&lt;instruction&gt;{...}&lt;/instruction&gt;&lt;code&gt;{full original file}&lt;/code&gt;&lt;update&gt;{edit with // ... existing code ... markers}&lt;/update&gt;</code> format; the model returns the fully merged file in <code>choices[0].message.content</code>. Use the <code>auto</code> model to let Morph pick between <code>morph-v3-fast</code> (10,500 tok/s, 96% accuracy, cheap) and <code>morph-v3-large</code> (5,000 tok/s, 98% accuracy, expensive). On OpenRouter the same models are published as <code>morph/morph-v3-fast</code> and <code>morph/morph-v3-large</code> with the rates above.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Step 3: Compact the chat history.</strong> When the conversation gets too long, POST to <code>/v1/compact</code> with <code>{ input: chatHistory, query: "what matters for the next LLM call", compression_ratio: 0.5, preserve_recent: 0 }</code>. The response includes <code>output</code> (the compressed text) and <code>compacted_line_ranges</code> showing exactly which lines survived. Sections wrapped in <code>&lt;keepContext&gt;</code> tags are preserved byte-for-byte regardless of compression ratio. The same operation is available as <code>model="morph-compactor"</code> on <code>/v1/chat/completions</code> for OpenAI-SDK-only integrations.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Step 4: Search the repo with WarpGrep.</strong> Pass <code>&lt;repo_structure&gt;{flat list of absolute paths}&lt;/repo_structure&gt;</code> + <code>&lt;search_string&gt;{natural language query}&lt;/search_string&gt;</code> to <code>model="morph-warp-grep-v2.1"</code>. The model returns <code>tool_calls</code> for <code>grep_search</code>, <code>read</code>, <code>list_directory</code>, <code>glob</code>, and <code>finish</code>; you execute each on the local filesystem and reply with <code>{role: "tool", tool_call_id, content}</code> messages. The loop typically runs 3-6 turns before <code>finish</code>. <strong>Important:</strong> you do <em>not</em> pass a <code>tools</code> array — the tools are built in.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Step 5: Monitor with Reflexes.</strong> On every agent turn, send the user message to <code>POST /v1/reflex/predict</code> with <code>model="jailbreak"</code>, <code>model="guardrail"</code>, <code>model="leaked-thinking"</code>, <code>model="stuck-in-a-loop"</code>, and <code>model="user-frustrated"</code>. Eleven built-in models cover the common failure modes; you can pass <code>models</code> as an array to run several in a single request. Each event bills at the per-event rate, with volume discounts past 1M events per billing month.</p>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4"><strong>Step 6: Route with the Model Router.</strong> Before each chat call, POST to <code>/v1/router/classify</code> with <code>{ input: prompt, classes: ["difficulty", "ambiguity", "domain"] }</code> (or use <code>/v1/router/multimodel</code> to get a fully-formed next-model recommendation). Map <code>difficulty: easy</code> to a cheap model, <code>difficulty: hard</code> to a flagship. The classifier signals include <code>confidence</code> and <code>meets_threshold</code>; treat low-confidence results as <code>needs_info</code> and fall back to your default model.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Claude Code, Codex, and Cursor integration</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Morph ships a single MCP server, <code>@morphllm/morphmcp</code>, that exposes <code>edit_file</code>, <code>codebase_search</code>, and <code>github_codebase_search</code> as native tools to any MCP-compatible agent. One command wires it into Claude Code:</p>

<pre class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 my-4 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>claude mcp add morph -- npx -y @morphllm/morphmcp@latest</code></pre>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Set <code>MORPH_API_KEY</code> in the env. The MCP server routes <code>edit_file</code> through Fast Apply (so the agent sends a partial edit and the merged file comes back), <code>codebase_search</code> through WarpGrep, and <code>github_codebase_search</code> through a GitHub-aware variant. For Cursor or Windsurf, add the same MCP server to your <code>mcp.json</code>. For raw Claude Code without MCP, the four Anthropic env vars route the chat loop through Morph on their own — useful when you want Morph for chat but still use Claude Code's built-in Read / Edit / Bash tools. Morph also publishes a one-command migration tool: <code>MORPH_API_KEY=*** npx -y @morphllm/morph-setup --kimi</code> drops the morph-migrate skill into your project, and <code>/morph-migrate</code> in Claude Code walks you through the conversion end-to-end.</p>

<h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-4">Morph vs OpenRouter, Together, Fireworks, DeepInfra, Nscale</h2>

<p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed my-4">Morph is not a pure chat-inference provider like <a href="/tutorials/openrouter-api-review-2026">OpenRouter</a>, <a href="/tutorials/together-ai-api-review">Together AI</a>, <a href="/tutorials/fireworks-api-review-2026">Fireworks</a>, <a href="/tutorials/deepinfra-api-review-2026">DeepInfra</a>, or <a href="/tutorials/nscale-serverless-inference-api-review-2026">Nscale</a>. The differentiation is the agent-specific endpoints, which none of those competitors ship:</p>

<table class="min-w-full border border-gray-300 my-4 text-sm">
<thead><tr class="bg-gray-100">
<th class="border p-2">Provider</th>
<th class="border p-2">Chat inference</th>
<th class="border p-2">Fast Apply</th>
